raw_text:
@xrqin  good catch on the verifier gap, you’re right that  hostStateHash  and  verifier  protect against different things, cross-state replay vs verifier substitution. 
 Fixed in the ref impl.  address verifier  now sits in  ClaimRelayRequest  between  proofDigest  and  hostStateHash , and the EIP-712 sig binds it so the relay can submit but can’t swap. 
 struct ClaimRelayRequest {
    bytes32 channelId;
    bytes32 lockId;
    bytes32 escrowCommitment;
    bytes32 outputCommitmentHash;
    uint256 maxRelayFee;
    uint256 deadline;
    bytes32 proofType;
    bytes32 proofDigest;
    address verifier;       // new, locked into typehash
    bytes32 hostStateHash;
    uint256 nonce;
}

 Updated EIP-712 type string: 
 ClaimRelayRequest(
  bytes32 channelId,
  bytes32 lockId,
  bytes32 escrowCommitment,
  bytes32 outputCommitmentHash,
  uint256 maxRelayFee,
  uint256 deadline,
  bytes32 proofType,
  bytes32 proofDigest,
  address verifier,
  bytes32 hostStateHash,
  uint256 nonce
)

 Commit:  Aboudjem/erc-8203-ref@48afc3a . 24/24 tests still green, will add a dedicated substitution-attack test next so the regression is explicit. 
 Worth a one-liner in the spec security considerations imo, something like  “the relay path MUST bind  verifier  in the signed request to prevent substitution on assembly” . Can open a PR for that if useful. 
 Also, the metered billing worked example is in decent shape, planning to drop it as a non-normative profile next week and will tag this thread. 

 @JackyWang  thanks for opening 28097, will drop a longer reply there. Short version on the batch question: each batch item as a separate  Job  feels right. The merkle settlement on 8203 is a gas-amortization trick, doesn’t change the unit-of-claim, so  JobAssurance  stays 1:1 and partial claims fall out naturally. 
 On dispute interop,  settleConditional ’s outcome ( proofType  +  proofDigest ) is the on-chain attestation your resolvers can consume directly.  IRiskHook  can hash against the digest. Can mock a flow if it helps.

